(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-05"] = {"phy-11-05-m01":{"idea":"Kepler's first law gives the shape of a planet's path and where the Sun sits.","steps":["Every planet moves on an ellipse.","The Sun is not at the middle of the ellipse. It is at one of the two foci.","A circle is only the special case where both foci meet."],"others":"A parabola is an open path, so a planet on it would not return. A circle with the Sun at the centre is too special, since real orbits are ellipses. An ellipse with the Sun at the centre is wrong, because the Sun sits at a focus.","trap":"Focus, not centre.","answer":"an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus"},"phy-11-05-m02":{"idea":"Equal areas in equal times means the planet must speed up when the radius becomes short.","steps":["The area swept in a short time is about ½ × r × v × t.","For the area per second to stay the same, r × v must stay the same.","So when r is small (close to the Sun), v is large."],"others":"A constant speed would make the swept area change as r changes. Moving faster when farther would break the equal area rule. Moving slower when closer is the reverse of the true behaviour.","trap":"This comes from conservation of angular momentum.","answer":"faster when it is closer to the Sun"},"phy-11-05-m03":{"idea":"Kepler's third law links the time of one orbit to the size of the orbit.","steps":["The law says T² is proportional to a³, where a is the semi-major axis.","For a circle, a is just the radius r.","So T² ∝ r³."],"others":"The mass of the planet does not appear in the law. The square of the semi-major axis would give a different power. The speed of the planet is not part of the law.","trap":"Square of time, cube of distance.","answer":"the cube of the semi-major axis of its orbit"},"phy-11-05-m04":{"idea":"Use T² ∝ r³, and compare with the Earth.","steps":["r is 4 times the Earth's. So T² = 4³ = 64 in units of (Earth years)².","T = √64 = 8.","The period is 8 years."],"others":"4 years would mean T is proportional to r, which is not true. 16 years would mean T goes with r², which is not the law. 2 years is √4, which uses the wrong power.","trap":"T ∝ r^(3/2). For r = 4, T = 4 × 2 = 8.","answer":"8"},"phy-11-05-m05":{"idea":"Newton's law of gravitation: every mass pulls every other mass. The pull falls with the square of the distance.","steps":["F = G m₁m₂ / r².","The force is always attractive.","G is the universal gravitational constant."],"others":"The force is attractive, never repulsive. Adding the masses is wrong, as the force depends on the product. A 1/r law is wrong, since the force goes with 1/r².","trap":"Product of masses on top. Square of distance below.","answer":"F = G m₁m₂/r², and it is attractive"},"phy-11-05-m06":{"idea":"The force goes as 1/r². Doubling r divides the force by 4.","steps":["F ∝ 1/r².","New distance = 2r, so F′ ∝ 1/(2r)² = 1/(4r²).","F′ = F/4."],"others":"Four times the force would need the distance to be halved. Twice the force and half the force would follow from a 1/r type of law, which is not correct here.","trap":"Double the distance, one fourth of the force.","answer":"one fourth of the original"},"phy-11-05-m07":{"idea":"Gravity is a mutual pull. Both bodies feel forces of the same size.","steps":["By Newton's third law, the Earth pulls the satellite with F.","The satellite pulls the Earth with a force of the same size, F, in the opposite direction.","They are an action-reaction pair."],"others":"The force on the Earth is not larger because the Earth is large. It is not zero. The Earth barely accelerates because of its huge mass, but the force is not smaller.","trap":"Same force on both. Different accelerations.","answer":"also F, as they are an action-reaction pair"},"phy-11-05-m08":{"idea":"Find the unit of G from F = G m₁m₂ / r².","steps":["G = F r² / (m₁ m₂).","The unit is N × m² / kg².","So G is in N m² kg⁻²."],"others":"N m² kg has the mass in the wrong place. N m kg⁻¹ uses r instead of r². N kg² m⁻² is upside down.","trap":"Check: N m² kg⁻² × kg² / m² gives N.","answer":"N m² kg⁻²"},"phy-11-05-m09":{"idea":"G is so small that its first laboratory measurement needed a very delicate set-up.","steps":["Henry Cavendish measured the pull between two big lead spheres and two small ones.","The small spheres hung from a torsion balance, a thin wire that twists.","The twist gave the force, and from it, G."],"others":"Newton used the Moon to test the law but did not measure G. Galileo studied falling bodies, not G. Kepler found the laws of orbits, not G.","trap":"Cavendish was the first to measure G in the laboratory.","answer":"Cavendish, using a torsion balance"},"phy-11-05-m10":{"idea":"The weight of a body at the surface equals the gravitational pull of the Earth on it.","steps":["mg = GMm/R².","The mass m cancels.","g = GM/R²."],"others":"GM/R has only one power of R. G/(MR²) has M in the wrong place. GM²/R has M squared and one power of R.","trap":"g depends on M and R, not on the mass of the falling body.","answer":"g = GM/R²"},"phy-11-05-m11":{"idea":"For a small height h, g goes down a little. Use the binomial approximation.","steps":["g_h = g R² / (R + h)² = g (1 + h/R)⁻².","For h much less than R: (1 + h/R)⁻² ≈ 1 − 2h/R.","So g_h ≈ g (1 − 2h/R)."],"others":"g(1 − h²/R²) has the wrong form. g(1 + 2h/R) goes up, but g falls with height. g(1 − h/R) is the formula for depth, not for height.","trap":"Height: 2h/R. Depth: d/R.","answer":"g(1 − 2h/R)"},"phy-11-05-m12":{"idea":"Below the surface, only the mass inside the radius R − d pulls the body.","steps":["With a uniform density, the inside mass goes as (R − d)³.","So g at depth d = g (R − d)/R.","g_d = g (1 − d/R)."],"others":"g(1 + d/R) would mean g goes up with depth. g R²/(R − d)² uses the law for outside the Earth. g(1 − 2d/R) is the formula for height with d in place of h.","trap":"Height: 1 − 2h/R. Depth: 1 − d/R.","answer":"g(1 − d/R)"},"phy-11-05-m13":{"idea":"Inside the Earth, g falls in a straight line from the surface to zero at the centre.","steps":["g_d = g (1 − d/R).","At the centre, d = R.","So g = 0 there."],"others":"g does not rise towards the centre, as less mass is pulling from inside. It is not 2g or infinite. It is not constant either, since it depends on d.","trap":"At the centre the pulls from all sides cancel.","answer":"decreases, and is zero at the centre"},"phy-11-05-m14":{"idea":"The rotation of the Earth acts against gravity most at the equator.","steps":["Points at the equator go on the largest circle, so the centripetal effect is largest there.","This reduces the effective g by Rω².","At the poles there is no such reduction. The Earth is flatter at the poles, which makes g slightly larger there."],"others":"At the poles the rotation has no effect, so g is the largest there. The flattening at the poles brings the surface closer to the centre and increases g there. The flattening does not reduce g at the equator in the way the rotation does.","trap":"g: smallest at the equator, largest at the poles.","answer":"the equator, as the rotation of the Earth reduces it most there"},"phy-11-05-m15":{"idea":"Gravitational potential energy is negative, since gravity is attractive and we put zero at infinity.","steps":["Work needed to bring the body from infinity to r is negative, since gravity does positive work.","U = −GMm/r.","It rises to zero as r goes to infinity."],"others":"−GMm/r² has the units of force. The positive forms +GMm/r and +GMm/r² would mean the energy is positive, which does not fit an attractive force with zero at infinity.","trap":"A bound body has negative total energy.","answer":"−GMm/r"},"phy-11-05-m16":{"idea":"To escape, the kinetic energy must at least cancel the negative potential energy.","steps":["½mv² = GMm/R.","With GM = gR², this becomes v² = 2gR.","v_e = √(2gR)."],"others":"2√(gR) has an extra factor and does not come from the energy balance. gR has the wrong units. √(gR) is the orbital speed near the surface, not the escape speed.","trap":"v_e = √2 × v_orbit.","answer":"v = √(2gR)"},"phy-11-05-m17":{"idea":"In ½mv² = GMm/R, the mass m cancels. So the escape speed does not depend on it.","steps":["½mv² = GMm/R.","Cancel the m on both sides.","v = √(2GM/R), which has no m."],"others":"It does not grow with the root of the mass. It does not fall with the mass. It does not grow in proportion to the mass.","trap":"Heavy or light, they all need 11.2 km/s.","answer":"the same, as it does not depend on the mass of the body"},"phy-11-05-m18":{"idea":"For a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal force.","steps":["mv²/r = GMm/r².","The mass m of the satellite cancels.","v = √(GM/r)."],"others":"A dependence on the satellite's mass is wrong, since m cancels. GM/r has no square root. √(2GM/r) is the escape speed at that distance.","trap":"Orbital speed is independent of the satellite's mass.","answer":"v = √(GM/r), which does not depend on the mass of the satellite"},"phy-11-05-m19":{"idea":"Use Kepler's third law, T² ∝ r³, for the period of a satellite.","steps":["T = 2πr / v and v = √(GM/r).","T = 2π r^(3/2) / √(GM).","So T is proportional to r^(3/2)."],"others":"T ∝ r would hold if the speed were constant. r^(1/2) is not found in the formula. T ∝ r² is not the law.","trap":"A higher orbit takes longer.","answer":"r^(3/2)"},"phy-11-05-m20":{"idea":"Weightlessness is not the absence of gravity. It is the absence of the contact force.","steps":["The astronaut and the satellite are in free fall with the same acceleration.","So the floor does not push on the astronaut.","The astronaut feels no weight, though gravity is still there."],"others":"At that height, gravity is still about 90 percent of its surface value. The force of gravity is not zero. The Moon's pull is much smaller and does not cancel the Earth's.","trap":"Weightless means feeling no push from the floor.","answer":"the astronaut and the satellite are both in free fall with the same acceleration"},"phy-11-05-m21":{"idea":"More speed than the circular speed, but less than the escape speed, gives an ellipse.","steps":["The satellite has too much speed to stay on the circle.","It moves outward, but it does not escape.","The orbit is an ellipse, and the starting point is the closest point, the perigee."],"others":"A spiral that ends on the Earth would need a loss of energy. A straight line away is the path for escape speed or more. A larger circle at the same speed is not possible, as the circular speed falls with radius.","trap":"Less than v_c: it falls inwards. Between v_c and v_e: ellipse.","answer":"an ellipse, with the starting point as the closest point to the Earth"},"phy-11-05-m22":{"idea":"A geostationary satellite seems fixed in the sky, as it turns with the Earth.","steps":["Its period equals one day, 24 hours.","It goes in the same direction as the Earth, west to east.","It must be in the equatorial plane, to stay above one point."],"others":"A period of 12 hours does not match the Earth's turn. 90 minutes is the period of a low orbit near the surface. A pole to pole orbit passes over every place and does not stay over one point.","trap":"Height is about 36 000 km above the equator.","answer":"24 hours, and it moves from west to east in the equatorial plane"},"phy-11-05-n01":{"idea":"Use Newton's law of gravitation.","steps":["F = G m₁m₂ / r².","= 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 10 × 20 / (1.0)².","= 1.334 × 10⁻⁸ N = 13.34 × 10⁻⁹ N."],"trap":"The force between everyday objects is extremely small.","answer":"13.34 (× 10⁻⁹ N)"},"phy-11-05-n02":{"idea":"Use g_h = g R² / (R + h)² for the exact value.","steps":["R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m and h = 3.2 × 10⁶ m, so R + h = 9.6 × 10⁶ m.","R/(R + h) = 6.4/9.6 = 2/3.","g_h = 9.8 × (2/3)² = 9.8 × 4/9 = 4.356 m/s²."],"trap":"The new g is less than half of 9.8, since the height is half of R.","answer":"4.356 m/s²"},"phy-11-05-n03":{"idea":"For a small h, use g_h = g (1 − 2h/R).","steps":["2h/R = 2 × 6.4 / 6400 = 0.002.","g_h = 9.8 × (1 − 0.002).","g_h = 9.8 × 0.998 = 9.78 m/s²."],"trap":"The drop is only 0.2 percent for 6.4 km.","answer":"9.78 m/s²"},"phy-11-05-n04":{"idea":"Below the surface, g falls in proportion to the distance from the centre.","steps":["g_d = g (1 − d/R), with d = 1.6 × 10⁶ m and R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m.","d/R = 0.25.","g_d = 9.8 × 0.75 = 7.35 m/s²."],"trap":"The value is less than at the surface. At d = R it would be zero.","answer":"7.35 m/s²"},"phy-11-05-n05":{"idea":"Use v_e = √(2gR).","steps":["v_e = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶).","= √(1.2544 × 10⁸).","= 1.12 × 10⁴ m/s = 11.2 km/s."],"trap":"11.2 km/s is the well-known escape speed of the Earth.","answer":"11.2 km/s"},"phy-11-05-n06":{"idea":"A satellite near the surface has r ≈ R, so v = √(gR).","steps":["v = √(9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶).","= √(6.272 × 10⁷).","= 7920 m/s = 7.92 km/s."],"trap":"It is the escape speed divided by √2: 11.2/1.414 = 7.92.","answer":"7.92 km/s"},"phy-11-05-n07":{"idea":"Use T = 2π √(r³/GM) with GM = gR².","steps":["The orbit radius is r = R + R = 2R = 1.28 × 10⁷ m.","T = 2π √(r³ / gR²) = 2π √(8R/g).","T = 2π √(8 × 6.4 × 10⁶ / 9.8) = 2π × 2286 = 14 360 s = 3.99 h."],"trap":"A near-surface satellite takes about 1.4 h. A higher orbit takes longer, so 3.99 h is sensible.","answer":"3.99 h"},"phy-11-05-n08":{"idea":"Use g = GM/R². Put in the new mass and radius.","steps":["M = 6.0 × 10²³ kg and R = 3.2 × 10⁶ m.","g = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 6.0 × 10²³ / (3.2 × 10⁶)².","= 4.002 × 10¹³ / 1.024 × 10¹³ = 3.91 m/s²."],"trap":"Another way: g = 9.8 × (1/10) / (1/2)² = 9.8 × 0.4 = 3.92. It is close, since 9.8 is a rounded value.","answer":"3.91 m/s²"},"phy-11-05-n09":{"idea":"Use Kepler's third law, T² ∝ r³, with the Earth as the reference.","steps":["r = 4 r_E, so T²/T_E² = 4³ = 64.","T/T_E = 8.","T = 8.0 years."],"trap":"The planet is slower: far planets have longer years.","answer":"8.0 years"},"phy-11-05-n10":{"idea":"At the surface, U = −GMm/R, with zero at infinity.","steps":["U = −6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 6.0 × 10²⁴ × 2.0 / (6.4 × 10⁶).","= −8.004 × 10¹⁴ / 6.4 × 10⁶.","= −1.25 × 10⁸ J."],"trap":"The sign is negative, since the body is bound to the Earth.","answer":"-1.25 × 10⁸ J"}};
